 {:seon.task/name "the-problems-step-holds-its-stall-until-it-sees-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Optimization. State 2026-11-11: both halves are on main (the step holds its eval on a fifo until main holds the stall; root's stall check armed by a host object, seon.step/timer, so a step that throws cannot leave it armed), and it hung once more: batch 32's first prove, the step past its bound at 83.7 s, green on the rerun. Evidence /tmp/claude-501/batch32-problems-overran/: the held agent's carrier in __open on the fifo (as intended), root's agent proc holding eval 7 with no effect out, no stall on main. :running is set by root's own agent proc when it sends the eval and cleared only by its :reply, so a trailing frame cannot drop the agent from root's busy set; left are a stall-check message that never came back (the timer then armed for good) or the busy set losing the agent another way. That evidence could not say which: root's ping answered only :agents :closing :pending until batch 34 (seon.system/stall-state now reads ::stall-armed ::busy ::lost and each agent proc's :stall-seen :last-frame-ms), so the next overrun's evidence names it. The wait itself ends honestly only by its producer's quiet (seon.check/a-wait-on-a-quiet-root-that-cannot-come-ends). Done when the cause is read from that evidence and fixed, and bin/check problems loops 5 of 5 on main. Earlier: Found 2026-11-10 by the managing session in batch 9's prove (main 19443302cc + readnowrite + day2pins), at machine load 26-32: the problems check step ran 77 minutes; its root (./seon problems --profile scripted, pid 93197) at 100% CPU and 13.7 GB resident, every thread parked but one in the collector (sweep_generation); one fiber running 22.2 G ticks under seon.check/run-check; three fiber dumps in its log. The same step on the same tree alone at load 5: PROBLEMS OK five of five, 10-15 s. Evidence: /tmp/claude-501/batch9-hang/ (problems.log, sample-93197.txt, batch9-prove.log, rerun*.log). Cause (read, the Tests session): seon.check/problems-check provokes a stall by an agent eval of (Thread/sleep 2500) under :stall-ms 1000 and then waits, unnamed, for a db value holding it: (seon.system/await-db sys #(seq (stalled-rows %))). The stall is :seon.process/stall, a fact present only while it is open (seon.problems/stalled-agents), asserted when root's stall timer (seon.system's :stall-check, a clock) finds the agent quiet past the bound, retracted by its next frame. Under load the timer's check can come after the sleep answered (no stall is ever recorded), or the assert and the retract can land in one writer batch (no db value holds it); either way no commit makes the predicate true, and an unnamed await-db ends only on such a commit: a wait with no end, re-running seon.problems/health on every commit of a root that keeps writing. Not the Datahike pin's: the wait on a transient fact is the step's, whatever the pin (the pin may change how often a batch holds both). Correct: the step holds the stall open until it has seen it, the agent's eval blocking on a fifo the step writes once the stall is on main (or on root's events, tapped before the submit), so the stall is certain at any load and the wait ends; the after-wait reads the retraction, which the release makes. Sweep: the other unnamed await-db waits in seon.check wait on work that completes (a vector proc's due set emptying). What made 13.7 GB is not read yet: the sample and dumps say a root re-running health per commit, which the fix removes; if it recurs elsewhere it is its own task. Proof, red first: the step with a stall bound longer than the sleep (no stall can come) ends, with a typed error naming the wait, never hangs; and green as today at load 5."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "test-inputs-sees-every-clock-read"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-10 (jolt/tests-cut): `bin/check test-inputs` (test/seon/check.clj test-inputs, docs/clocks.md) does not see a read of System/currentTimeMillis, one of its own hidden-inputs: a probe test file holding (defn probe-a [] (System/currentTimeMillis)) left :unnamed [] and TEST-INPUTS OK, and two real reads went unnamed (seon.results-test's age!, seon.e2e-test/await-quiet-ends-on-a-recorded-defect; both replaced on that branch). System/getenv reads in seon.config-test are seen, so the hole is in how that call's :form/refs are recorded or queried, not read yet. A clock read in a string sent to an agent or root (Thread/sleep in seon.agent-test/a-proof-run-ends-with-its-agent and seon.repl-test/a-reply-comes-after-its-commit) is no form ref at all. Correct: the check reads what the compiler records of every hidden input, so a probe of each one in hidden-inputs is red. Proof, red first: a check of test-inputs over a probe file reading each hidden input names each one."
