 {:seon.task/name "a-wait-on-a-quiet-root-that-cannot-come-ends"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Optimization. Every wait ends (AGENTS.md section 4); a wait on a predicate that can never become true ended only by bin/check's step bound (batch 24: the mcp check's await-records on an eval record :seon.eval/by \"outside\", which step 2 made a ref, hung 51.6 s twice). DONE (jolt/quietwait, 2026-11-11): await-records is deleted. An agent commits a request's writes before it replies (seon.repl/agent-step), so the mcp check and the two repl tests that waited on :commit events read the branch once from the store (seon.db/head) after the answer: no wait is left that a record that never comes could hang (docs/machine.md#builds-and-checks). A named seon.system/await-db already ends at the reconciler's quiet with :seon.system/quiet-before-true. LEFT: the UNNAMED await-db waits on facts another proc makes, which only a commit ends: the problems step's stall (test/seon/check.clj problems-check: during and after, the batch 32 overrun) and the vectors' due (four sites: scale, idle, pages, vector-check). The reconciler's quiet says nothing of the api's stall timer or the vectors proc. The honest end is each producer's own quiet: the vectors proc answering 'nothing due and nothing in flight' as the reconciler answers :quiet, and the stall check answering a :stall? it judged not a stall (the agent proc's verdict, sent back) so the step can end on 'checked past the bound, no stall' as a typed error, never a clock. FOUND 2026-11-11 (jolt/probwait): a wait's predicate on main runs IN MAIN'S WRITER at every commit (seon.system/await-db's watch; Datahike's writer resets the connection), so a predicate that rebuilds something over the whole db holds every commit behind it: the problems step's whole-report waits did (batches 32 and 38; fixed: one class and the reconciler's quiet). The same shape remains in test/seon/e2e.clj:259 (the reconciler's whole index per commit) and test/seon/check.clj's four seon.vector/due waits (all of main's texts per commit): each reads the one fact it waits on, or waits for its producer's quiet. Proof: the problems step with its stall check disabled ends red with that error instead of hanging; and a cost proof per wait that counts its predicate's whole-db reads over commits that do not satisfy it (as seon.check-test/the-problems-steps-waits-read-no-whole-report-per-commit)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-selection-derives-what-it-needs-from-its-steps"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-11 (Deep fixes' one red in 23, its own hand-made selection; Point): (1) bin/check decides whether a run needs the binary and the seeded store (NEEDS, bin/check near line 77) from a file of the selection (an empty steps-binary reads as nothing needs it), not from the steps selected, so durable ran on an empty, unseeded store and failed two phases later; derive it from the steps (seon.proofs/step-needs-binary?), or refuse the mismatch. Reproduction (two sessions lost hours to it, 2026-11-10 and 11, as reported): a selection written by hand with an empty steps-binary, as bin/test writes one for its fast tier (mkdir D; echo durable > D/steps; : > D/steps-binary; bin/check --steps D) seeds no store, wants no build and gives durable an empty store, which fails far from the cause; D holding only steps seeds and uses the binary (no steps-binary is unknown, never none). Until this lands, docs/checks.md#running-steps-by-hand is the one way by hand. (2) durable-write says OK when its evals failed: test/seon/check.clj's p1 drops the eval's error and the check reads only :merged; fail the write phase where the cause is. The class of seon.check-test/a-selection-runs-every-step-it-names-and-refuses-one-it-cannot: a check that quietly ran something else. Proof: bin/check --steps with durable selected and an empty steps-binary is CHECK FAILED naming it; a durable-write whose eval fails is red at the write. Owner: Tests."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-checks-own-workers-are-never-counted-as-roots"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Optimization. Found 2026-11-11 in batch 33's first prove: the scale step red on :root-threads-as-declared, :after-work {:os [47 48]} (the roster equal, the OS count one more), green alone and on the rerun (log .worktrees/batch-33/target/check/20261111T083330-92782/scale.log). Read, not reproduced: the check runs every agent's work through test/seon/check.clj's fan, a FixedThreadPool of 32 OS threads, and measures root's threads right after; fan's shutdown and awaitTermination return once the tasks finished, while a worker can still be exiting at the OS level, out of Jolt's roster and still counted by seon.lib/nthreads. Not seon.lib/sh's (batch 32): for a caller on a fiber it is clojure.java.shell/sh unchanged, and the old path's threads were named, which the roster would show. Correct: fan runs its work on fibers (jolt.fibers spawn and join; every wait in it parks), so the check makes no OS thread of its own; fan existed because pmap's futures linger. Proof: scale's :root-threads-as-declared green at n=100 under a concurrent prove, repeatedly, and fan starts no thread (a thread count around it)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}

 {:seon.task/name "health-reads-a-base-of-another-program-schema"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-11 by the Tests session (jolt/tests-healthbase). Health builds the base's program with the base's own code in its own process, and the head reads the base's facts: a branch that changes the program's own attributes (:ns/, :var/, :form/ or the store's layout) is a typed HEALTH FAILED, since the head cannot read a base of another program schema. Before, the head analyzed the base with its own code, which collided across a namespace rename. Correct: the base's process answers health's base-side reads itself (what health asks of the base: its causes and classes), so no image reads another schema's store. Proof: a branch adding a :form/ attribute gets HEALTH OK."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/health-reads-a-base-of-another-program-schema}} {:seon.task/name "a-check-ends-its-timers-with-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: tests. Found 2026-11-12 (root): a bin/check whose prove ended red left a process of its own behind, pgid 30592, sleeping its 600 s checkpoint timer after the run was over; root ended it by hand. Cause (read in bin/check): the checkpoint timer (the subshell `sleep ${SEON_CHECK_EVIDENCE_AFTER_S:-600}`, W) and each unit's bound watcher (bounded) are ended only on the normal path after `wait`; the EXIT trap removes the starter fifo and the results dir and ends neither, so a run ended by TERM (its TERM trap exits 143) orphans them. Plan: the EXIT trap ends every timer the run started (W and the watchers bounded records), so whatever ends the run ends what it started; nothing else changes. Estimate: two kills at exit, ~0 work. Proof, red first: a bin/check selection sent TERM a second after it starts leaves no process whose parent was it (ps by parent before and after)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-check-ended-by-term-leaves-nothing-running}}
